hat category, weighted by its own confidence, clamped to 0..100 -- and published as trust, 100 minus that accumulation, so a higher published number always means more trustworthy.","Signals are summed in ascending code order so the result does not depend on the order data arrived in.","A category whose data could not be gathered is held at or below a published trust ceiling of 65 and raises INSUFFICIENT_DATA; missing data never raises a trust score.","The published overall trust score is 100 minus the weighted mean of the risk accumulations of the categories this scan actually measured, renormalized to their own combined weight -- a category held at the uncertainty floor does not otherwise pull the headline number toward it -- then held at or below 40 if any scoring signal is high severity and at or below 20 if any is critical. If nothing could be measured, overall sits at the same uncertainty ceiling as any other unmeasured category.","`measuredWeight` (0..1, alongside `confidence`) is the share of the category weight that fed the overall score; a reader should trust a headline number in proportion to it, since a low risk built on a small measured slice is not the same claim as a low risk built on all seven.","Scores are trust, not a quality rating: 100 means nothing risky was observed, 0 means the worst observable state. They are never a recommendation.","Scores from the probabilistic layer -- wallet clustering, Sybil and insider grouping, wash-trading likelihood and creator fingerprinting -- are snapped to the nearest 5 before they are scored, so that independent implementations of those heuristics can agree on a value without coordinating on one. Every signal marked probabilistic in this report was rounded that way; the deterministic core was not.","Categories scored at the uncertainty floor in this run: creator, holder, liquidity."],"scoring":"Each signal contributes points to one risk category internally, weighted by its own confidence; category risk is combin